{"kind":"request","text":"Stop test files from changing the shipped stylesheet. ui/src/index.css line 3 is a bare @import tailwindcss with the Vite plugin and no @source directive, so Tailwind v4 auto-detects what to scan and ui/test is inside the scanned set. It scans text, not the import graph, so any occurrence of a utility name anywhere in those files emits the rule. This is not hypothetical: while closing the personal-work storage harness, the word transform inside a comment in a test file emitted a .transform rule into the shipped CSS and broke make ui-check. The implementer reworded the comment rather than commit a changed bundle, which is the right call for that round and the wrong fix in general. A prose comment in a test should not be able to alter what users download, and the failure arrives as a byte-difference in a generated bundle, which reads as a stale rebuild rather than as what it is.","body":{"conditions":"Tailwind scans only the application sources. Adding a comment containing a utility class name to a file under ui/test leaves a fresh make ui build byte-identical to the committed embed, demonstrated by making that edit and rebuilding. The application's own styles are unchanged: the committed embed at the new head is byte-identical to the one at its parent apart from any deliberate removal of rules that only ui/test was keeping alive, and each such removal is named in the report. Full UI suite, tsc, npm build, make ui-check, go vet and go test green. Approved at an exact head by a reviewer other than the author.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}